Abstraction is the hiding of the implementation of different methods in a program. It refers to displaying only the functionalities of a program while hiding how
the program achieves those functionalities. 
It requires Interface where differnet abstract methods are declared which reflects the functionalities of a program. 
Then a class inherits a Is-A relationship with this interface using implements keyword.
The next step is to override all the methods of the interface to provide an implementation or logic to them so that they can perform their functions. This implementation
of the methods is what we have to hide in Abstraction.
The last step is Upcasting. In this process the overrided methods are called via super class reference of subclass types.
Upon ctrl+clicking the methods the cursor moves to superclass methods(abstract methods in interface), where we can see only the declaration of the methods. We cannot see
the implementation of the methods. This is Abstraction.

Upon executing the program, the JVM checks that the method belongs to which class.
Parent p = new Son();
p.sonStudies();

The p reference stores an object of Son class so when the method is called using it, the second statement becomes, new Son().sonStudies. This directs the JVM to the 
sub class Son and invokes the method. This sub class stores the implementation of the methods as well. This implementation was hidden while checking the methods with
ctrl+click. It is checked at runtime by JVM, this is called as Run time polymorphism/late binding or dynamic binding.
